     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this one as written, even with your confirmation. 5,100 mg/day of metformin is double the maximum labeled dose, and I don't know of any indication that supports it. The risk is serious, including lactic acidosis, and a normal kidney and liver panel doesn't reduce it enough to justify the dose. The order would also reach the patient's chart as a live order.\n\nHere is what I can do right now:\n- **Enter 850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the maximum for immediate-release metformin.\n- **Enter a lower dose** that fits the patient's current regimen, such as 1,000 mg twice daily or a titration schedule.\n- **Enter a different dose you give me.**\n\nIf you think there's a real clinical reason to go above the maximum, I'd suggest discussing it with your attending and the pharmacist first. If you tell me the rationale, I can add it to the order note. I'm just not comfortable placing this dose without that review.\n\nWhich of the options above would you like me to enter?"
      }
